#{[17592186484169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484169 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484169 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484169 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186484000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484169 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aa4e5556-e66f-5ee1-bddc-c98cca0920b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484179 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cd48dcdf-ec5c-553b-93df-1ad0ce12e474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484225 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484225 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484225 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{116}$ be a random sample of size $n=116$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $3$ and $5.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5574158c-e378-5c22-867d-7f3ecb6e0b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484225 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=116,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.6+5\\cdot 0.1+10\\cdot 0.2+15\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+0.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{5}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{15}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.5+20+22.5)-16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{29}{116}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5) & ≈P(\\frac{3-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{5-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}) \\\\  & =P(-2<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5)=Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484253 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66,$ calculate $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0606\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0594\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0582\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0735\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0721\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0708\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0885\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0869\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0853\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7088\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7123\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7157\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7422\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7454\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7486\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7734\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7764\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7794\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95a41c31-9a32-5733-b64d-135e1d1b45d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484253 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{45}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{128}{45})-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{45}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.2,\\frac{0.66}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.2,0.022).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =P(\\frac{2-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}<Z<\\frac{2.3-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.35<Z<0.67) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.67)-P(Z<-1.35) \\\\  & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.67)=0.7486, \\\\ Φ(-1.35)=0.0885.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35) \\\\  & =0.7486-0.0885 \\\\  & =0.6601.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484259 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484259 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484259 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{121}$ be a random sample of size $n=121$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =300$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =55.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $295.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2c4c6dc-06db-5c59-88cb-4cad1074fe93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484259 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=121,\\,\\mu =300,\\,\\sigma =55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(300,\\frac{{55}^{2}}{121}) \\\\  & ∼N(300,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<295).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =P(Z<\\frac{295-300}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1) \\\\  & =Φ(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1)=0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =Φ(-1) \\\\  & =0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484262 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484262 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{65}$ be a random sample of size $n=65$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $-4.9.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7a75f10-4331-5d53-8955-dd0d91fe7458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484262 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=65,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 0.1+(-6)\\cdot 0.4+(-4)\\cdot 0.4+(-2)\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.8-2.4-1.6-0.2 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.1+(-6)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-(-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(6.4+14.4+6.4+0.4)-25 \\\\  & =2.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(-5,\\frac{2.6}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-4.9-(-5)}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.5) \\\\  & =Φ(0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9)=Φ(0.5).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484332 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484332 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e72641b-14ce-5981-bb0d-51d727a8f321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484332 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484336 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b6a43a1-9e1f-512b-801a-9425c6c455d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484336 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186484259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484345 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba1b50f8-7d